> that is what was failing on ppc64.

I'm not really sure what it's supposed to look like when "perf =20
annotate" works.  It spits a bunch of unreadable[1] dark-blue-on-black =20
assembly code at me, all with "0.00 :" in the left column.

Oh, wait -- some lines have "100.00 : " on the left, in =20
even-more-unreadable dark-red-on-black.

Apart from the annoying colors, is there anything specific I should be =20
looking for?  Some sort of error message, or output that actually makes =20
sense?

I've attached the output from "perf annotate" and "perf report".  =20
perf.data was generated by "perf record find /usr > /dev/null" on an =20
NFS root (which took a few seconds to complete), so the large amount of =20
__alloc_skb makes some sense, but the way perf annotate shows 100% on =20
one instruction in each function seems odd.

-Scott

[1] ...unless I crank the brightness up on my monitor to the point =20
where whites are blinding, or redirect the output to a file so the =20
colors go away.=

